**Confirmation and access**

The data subject has the right to obtain from the controller confirmation as to whether or not personal data concerning him or her are being processed and, where that is the case, access to the personal data [gdpr#art15]. Recital 63 states that a data subject should have the right of access to personal data collected concerning him or her and to exercise that right easily and at reasonable intervals, in order to be aware of and verify the lawfulness of the processing [gdpr#rec63].

**The specific information items**

Where personal data are being processed, the controller must provide the following information [gdpr#art15]:

- (a) the purposes of the processing [gdpr#art15];
- (b) the categories of personal data concerned [gdpr#art15];
- (c) the recipients or categories of recipient to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, in particular recipients in third countries or international organisations [gdpr#art15];
- (d) where possible, the envisaged period for which the personal data will be stored, or, if not possible, the criteria used to determine that period [gdpr#art15];
- (e) the existence of the right to request from the controller rectification or erasure of personal data or restriction of processing of personal data concerning the data subject, or to object to such processing [gdpr#art15];
- (f) the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority [gdpr#art15];
- (g) where the personal data are not collected from the data subject, any available information as to their source [gdpr#art15];
- (h) the existence of automated decision-making, including profiling, referred to in Article 22(1) and (4) and, at least in those cases, meaningful information about the logic involved, as well as the significance and the envisaged consequences of such processing for the data subject [gdpr#art15].

Recital 63 similarly refers to the right to know and obtain communication in particular with regard to the purposes for which the personal data are processed, where possible the period for which they are processed, the recipients, the logic involved in any automatic personal data processing and, at least when based on profiling, the consequences of such processing [gdpr#rec63].

**Transfers to third countries**

Where personal data are transferred to a third country or to an international organisation, the data subject has the right to be informed of the appropriate safeguards pursuant to Article 46 relating to the transfer [gdpr#art15].

**Copy of the data and form of provision**

The controller shall provide a copy of the personal data undergoing processing, and for any further copies requested by the data subject the controller may charge a reasonable fee based on administrative costs [gdpr#art15]. Where the data subject makes the request by electronic means, and unless otherwise requested by the data subject, the information shall be provided in a commonly used electronic form [gdpr#art15]. The right to obtain a copy shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of others [gdpr#art15]; recital 63 adds that this right should not adversely affect the rights or freedoms of others, including trade secrets or intellectual property, but that this should not result in a refusal to provide all information to the data subject [gdpr#rec63].

**Modalities of provision**

Information under Articles 15 to 22 must be provided in a concise, transparent, intelligible and easily accessible form, using clear and plain language, in writing or by other means including electronic means, and orally when requested by the data subject provided that identity is proven by other means [gdpr#art12-p1]. Information and actions under Articles 15 to 22 must be provided free of charge, subject to the manifestly unfounded or excessive request exception [gdpr#art12-p1]. The controller must provide information on action taken without undue delay and in any event within one month of receipt of the request, extendable by two further months where necessary [gdpr#art12-p1].
